1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,431
The certificate expired at midnight.

2
00:00:03,431 --> 00:00:06,405
Nobody knew it was bound to the payments virtual

3
00:00:06,405 --> 00:00:09,007
server. By the time the first alert fired,

4
00:00:09,007 --> 00:00:10,680
the queue was already full.

5
00:00:11,430 --> 00:00:14,135
What if an agent had found it weeks earlier,

6
00:00:14,135 --> 00:00:17,516
planned the renewal across every load balancer you own,

7
00:00:17,516 --> 00:00:20,897
opened the change ticket, then waited for your approval

8
00:00:20,897 --> 00:00:22,864
before touching a single device?

9
00:00:22,864 --> 00:00:25,692
That is certificate renewal, done with Claude,

10
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:26,430
done safely.

11
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:31,404
Gennoor Tech presents Certificate Renewal Automation with Claude.

12
00:00:32,154 --> 00:00:34,897
Every platform. One renewal pipeline.

13
00:00:34,897 --> 00:00:38,456
F5, NetScaler, Radware and other load balancers,

14
00:00:38,456 --> 00:00:41,940
handled by one plan, one approval and one audit

15
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:45,721
trail. You build it in nine increments across three

16
00:00:45,721 --> 00:00:48,464
days, each one something you can run,

17
00:00:48,464 --> 00:00:51,207
review and demonstrate. Claude plans,

18
00:00:51,207 --> 00:00:54,988
drafts and explains. Deterministic Python executes.

19
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:57,954
A person approves. Everything is logged.

20
00:00:58,704 --> 00:01:02,194
You start with the certificate lifecycle as a load

21
00:01:02,194 --> 00:01:05,545
balancer actually sees it. Client S S L profiles

22
00:01:05,545 --> 00:01:07,359
and virtual servers on F5.

23
00:01:07,359 --> 00:01:10,361
Cert-key objects and bindings on NetScaler.

24
00:01:10,361 --> 00:01:14,130
Certificate repositories and S S L policies on Radware

25
00:01:14,130 --> 00:01:17,062
Alteon. You renew one certificate by hand,

26
00:01:17,062 --> 00:01:20,482
write down every step, and mark every point where

27
00:01:20,482 --> 00:01:23,553
a wrong chain, a mismatched key or a skipped

28
00:01:23,553 --> 00:01:26,136
H A sync would have caused an outage.

29
00:01:26,886 --> 00:01:29,175
Then you set up your workbench.

30
00:01:29,175 --> 00:01:32,645
Claude Code with a project file that knows your

31
00:01:32,645 --> 00:01:34,639
naming rules and red lines.

32
00:01:34,639 --> 00:01:37,813
The Claude Agent S D K running Python tools

33
00:01:37,813 --> 00:01:40,176
with structured J S O N outputs.

34
00:01:40,176 --> 00:01:43,720
An M C P server that exposes read-only inventory

35
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:46,304
calls and nothing else. After that,

36
00:01:46,304 --> 00:01:49,405
a discovery agent pulls every certificate,

37
00:01:49,405 --> 00:01:52,802
expiry date and binding from F5 iControl REST,

38
00:01:52,802 --> 00:01:54,722
the NetScaler NITRO A P I,

39
00:01:54,722 --> 00:01:57,380
the Radware Alteon REST A P I and an

40
00:01:57,380 --> 00:01:59,816
adapter for other load balancers,

41
00:01:59,816 --> 00:02:01,662
into one typed inventory.

42
00:02:02,412 --> 00:02:05,008
On day two the planning agent arrives.

43
00:02:05,008 --> 00:02:09,175
It reads the inventory, groups certificates by expiry window,

44
00:02:09,175 --> 00:02:12,523
owner and blast radius, and drafts a renewal plan

45
00:02:12,523 --> 00:02:14,026
as structured J S O N.

46
00:02:14,026 --> 00:02:16,758
It must cite the inventory rows it used;

47
00:02:16,758 --> 00:02:20,242
a missing owner or an unverified binding blocks the

48
00:02:20,242 --> 00:02:22,360
plan rather than being guessed.

49
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:26,049
Then it opens the change record through the ServiceNow

50
00:02:26,049 --> 00:02:28,030
Table A P I and hands it to a

51
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:30,831
Flow Designer approval path. The C S R is

52
00:02:30,831 --> 00:02:34,042
generated by code, and the private key is never

53
00:02:34,042 --> 00:02:35,340
shown to the model.

54
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:40,619
Then the executors. Deterministic, tested Python that uploads,

55
00:02:40,619 --> 00:02:44,417
installs and binds the certificate on each platform.

56
00:02:44,417 --> 00:02:46,973
On F5 that is iControl REST and t m

57
00:02:46,973 --> 00:02:49,457
s h. On NetScaler, the NITRO A P I

58
00:02:49,457 --> 00:02:52,671
with primary-only writes and a saved config.

59
00:02:52,671 --> 00:02:55,447
On Radware, the Alteon REST A P I with

60
00:02:55,447 --> 00:02:57,857
apply and save as explicit steps.

61
00:02:57,857 --> 00:03:01,728
For other load balancers you build an adapter against

62
00:03:01,728 --> 00:03:05,527
the same contract. Claude writes the first draft and

63
00:03:05,527 --> 00:03:09,836
explains every call. Then pytest proves it against recorded

64
00:03:09,836 --> 00:03:13,050
device responses before it touches hardware.

65
00:03:13,050 --> 00:03:15,753
On day three the guardrails close in:

66
00:03:15,753 --> 00:03:19,186
an approved change, an open maintenance window,

67
00:03:19,186 --> 00:03:21,158
a device on the allow list,

68
00:03:21,158 --> 00:03:23,568
H A in sync. Miss any one and the

69
00:03:23,568 --> 00:03:26,490
install is refused with a logged reason.

70
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:30,324
Validation comes next. A T L S handshake test

71
00:03:30,324 --> 00:03:33,134
on every virtual server before and after.

72
00:03:33,134 --> 00:03:35,532
Chain, key match and serial checks.

73
00:03:35,532 --> 00:03:39,027
If the post-check fails, the executor rolls back to

74
00:03:39,027 --> 00:03:42,180
the previous certificate object automatically,

75
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:44,784
and you will watch it do exactly that.

76
00:03:45,534 --> 00:03:48,535
Finally you wire the whole thing into a GitHub

77
00:03:48,535 --> 00:03:51,014
pull request and a GitHub Actions run,

78
00:03:51,014 --> 00:03:53,884
so every renewal plan is reviewed like code,

79
00:03:53,884 --> 00:03:57,211
with tests, dry run and post-check report attached.

80
00:03:57,211 --> 00:04:00,995
Renewals are scheduled ahead of expiry and the certificate

81
00:04:00,995 --> 00:04:02,430
owner gets the report.

82
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:05,382
Every lab runs against lab devices,

83
00:04:05,382 --> 00:04:08,275
a lab certificate authority and recorded A P I

84
00:04:08,275 --> 00:04:10,917
responses, so you can break things safely.

85
00:04:10,917 --> 00:04:13,559
You will watch a renewal get blocked for a

86
00:04:13,559 --> 00:04:16,263
missing approval, break a chain on purpose,

87
00:04:16,263 --> 00:04:18,276
and roll back against the clock.

